{
  "rules": [
    "Early-horizon (≈days 1–18): model has a consistent positive bias (~+0.3 to +2 EUR) and tends to overestimate during dips; apply a small downward shift in the first half of the path.",
    "Mid-horizon around local troughs (≈days 10–16): model overshoots rebounds (predicts higher than realized) and then follows with too-deep a pullback; dampen short-term oscillations (reduce amplitude) rather than alternating over/under moves.",
    "Late-horizon (≈days 23–30): model systematically underestimates strong uptrends/rallies (often by ~2–10 EUR) and fails to capture breakout persistence; add an upward trend/rally persistence adjustment in the final third of the horizon.",
    "After upside breakouts/spikes: model mean-reverts too quickly (forecasts flatten/decline) while realized prices keep grinding higher; slow the mean-reversion speed and extend momentum when recent returns are positive and accelerating.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias rule: use a piecewise bias correction—downward bias correction for days 1–18, near-neutral for days 19–22, upward bias correction increasing with horizon for days 23–30."
  ]
}
